Grant Gustafson writes:
%>>%>Figure environment and floats in particular currently behave in a way
%>>%>that makes page design inconvenient. Programs like PageMaker look
%>>%>attractive compared with LaTeX. Even stupid programs like MS-Word and
%>>%>WordPerfect look attractive compared with LaTeX.
%>>
%>>Clearly someone who **never** typesets mathematics...
%>
%>Dear Mike,
%>
%>I will respond to the above statement by the following:
%>
%>Example: Margo Mankus, degree from UIUC Illinois, applied math
%>
%>         Writes her papers and thesis in LaTeX
%>
%>         Two years later, chooses MS-word to write her book!
%>         Why? Because LaTeX made the page design too clumsy to get
%>              anything done in a reasonable length of time! She had
%>              2 or 3 figures per page and a limited amount of time
%>              available to write the book (187 pages, calculus).
%>
%>        Her husband, Frank Wattenberg, is writing his book using LaTeX,
%>        and likely will admit that Margo got done quickly (he's not done
%>        yet - calculus reform text).
%>

When you say *write* do you mean that this was camera-ready copy, or that a
MS-Word document was used for typesetting; or do you mean that the thing was
typed and then typeset as a separate operation?

I devised a simple document here that was used to help the Maths School
decide which system to use. (The decision to use TeX was forced upon many of
us by its support by the various mathematical associations anyway, but this
was a sort of feasibility study.) One of our programmers had first chosen a
page from a calculus book, in which the most sophisticated use of maths was
the use of a partial derivative \partial and an integration \int. I decided
this was not subtle enough, and chose the following. Someone *did* type it in
in WordPerfect, but it looked a complete and utter mess. He also complained
that many of the blackboard bold symbols seemed to be missing. The Word
protagonists didn't even produce anything.

Some of our mathematicians use chiwriter too, and swear by it, but none of
the pure mathematicians use anything other than TeX.

Mike

